        "item": {
          "clientId": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"2. Show that there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers $(m, n)$ such that\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{m+1}{n}+\\\\frac{n+1}{m}\\n$$\\nis a positive integer.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"british_mo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidates\": [{\"candidate_id\": \"choice-1\", \"text\": \"Let\\n\\\\[\\nS(m,n)=\\\\frac{m+1}{n}+\\\\frac{n+1}{m}.\\n\\\\]\\nWe construct infinitely many positive integer pairs for which \\\\(S(m,n)=4\\\\).\\n\\nDefine positive integers \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) recursively by\\n\\\\[\\n(t_0,y_0)=(1,1),\\\\qquad\\n t_{k+1}=2t_k+3y_k,\\\\quad y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k.\\n\\\\]\\nA direct calculation gives\\n\\\\[\\nt_{k+1}^2-3y_{k+1}^2=t_k^2-3y_k^2.\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(t_0^2-3y_0^2=-2\\\\), induction yields\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2-3y_k^2=-2 \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nfor every \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\). Also, induction shows that \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) are positive odd integers. Moreover,\\n\\\\[\\ny_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k>y_k,\\n\\\\]\\nso the \\\\(y_k\\\\) are all distinct.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\nn_k=\\\\frac{y_k+1}{2}.\\n\\\\]\\nThen \\\\(n_k\\\\) is a positive integer, and (1) implies\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2=3y_k^2-2=12n_k^2-12n_k+1. \\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nNow define\\n\\\\[\\nm_k=\\\\frac{4n_k-1+t_k}{2}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause \\\\(t_k\\\\) is odd, \\\\(m_k\\\\) is an integer. It is positive since all its terms are positive. Equation (2) says that \\\\(t_k^2\\\\) is the discriminant of\\n\\\\[\\nm^2+(1-4n_k)m+(n_k^2+n_k)=0.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(m_k\\\\), given by the quadratic formula, satisfies\\n\\\\[\\nm_k^2+m_k+n_k^2+n_k=4m_kn_k.\\n\\\\]\\nDividing by \\\\(m_kn_k>0\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m_k+1}{n_k}+\\\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4,\\n\\\\]\\na positive integer.\\n\\nFinally, the \\\\(n_k\\\\) are strictly increasing, so the pairs \\\\((m_k,n_k)\\\\) are all distinct. Hence there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers \\\\((m,n)\\\\) with the required property.\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-2\", \"text\": \"Define integers \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) by\\n\\\\[\\n(t_0,y_0)=(1,1),\\\\qquad t_{k+1}=2t_k+3y_k,\\\\quad y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k.\\n\\\\]\\nInductively, \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) are positive odd integers. Moreover,\\n\\\\[\\nt_{k+1}^2-3y_{k+1}^2=(2t_k+3y_k)^2-3(t_k+2y_k)^2=t_k^2-3y_k^2.\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(t_0^2-3y_0^2=-2\\\\), we have\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2-3y_k^2=-2\\n\\\\]\\nfor every \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\). Also \\\\(y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k>y_k\\\\), so the \\\\(y_k\\\\) are distinct.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\nn_k=\\\\frac{y_k+1}{2},\\\\qquad m_k=\\\\frac{2y_k+1+t_k}{2}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) are positive and odd, \\\\(m_k,n_k\\\\) are positive integers. From \\\\(y_k=2n_k-1\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2=3y_k^2-2=12n_k^2-12n_k+1.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(t_k^2\\\\) is the discriminant of\\n\\\\[\\nX^2+(1-4n_k)X+n_k^2+n_k=0,\\n\\\\]\\nand its larger root is\\n\\\\[\\nX=\\\\frac{4n_k-1+t_k}{2}=m_k.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently,\\n\\\\[\\nm_k^2+n_k^2+m_k+n_k-4m_kn_k=0.\\n\\\\]\\nDividing by \\\\(m_kn_k>0\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m_k+1}{n_k}+\\\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4,\\n\\\\]\\na positive integer. Since the integers \\\\(n_k=(y_k+1)/2\\\\) are strictly increasing, the pairs \\\\((m_k,n_k)\\\\) are all distinct. Hence there are infinitely many such pairs of positive integers.\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-3\", \"text\": \"Let\\n\\\\[\\n(t_0,y_0)=(1,1),\\\\qquad t_{k+1}=2t_k+3y_k,\\\\qquad y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k.\\n\\\\]\\nWe claim that, for every \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2-3y_k^2=-2.\\n\\\\]\\nThis holds initially, and\\n\\\\[\\n(2t+3y)^2-3(t+2y)^2=t^2-3y^2,\\n\\\\]\\nso it is preserved by the recurrence. Moreover, \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) are positive odd integers: this is true initially, and the recurrence preserves positivity and oddness. Also\\n\\\\(y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k>y_k\\\\), so the \\\\(y_k\\\\) are strictly increasing.\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\nn_k=\\\\frac{y_k+1}{2},\\\\qquad m_k=\\\\frac{4n_k-1+t_k}{2}.\\n\\\\]\\nBoth are positive integers, since \\\\(y_k\\\\) and \\\\(t_k\\\\) are odd. From \\\\(y_k=2n_k-1\\\\) and \\\\(t_k^2-3y_k^2=-2\\\\), we obtain\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2=3(2n_k-1)^2-2=12n_k^2-12n_k+1.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(t_k^2\\\\) is the discriminant of\\n\\\\[\\nm^2+(1-4n_k)m+(n_k^2+n_k)=0,\\n\\\\]\\nand the quadratic formula shows that \\\\(m_k\\\\) is a root. Hence\\n\\\\[\\nm_k^2+n_k^2+m_k+n_k-4m_kn_k=0.\\n\\\\]\\nSince \\\\(m_k,n_k>0\\\\), division by \\\\(m_kn_k\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m_k+1}{n_k}+\\\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4,\\n\\\\]\\na positive integer. Finally, the strictly increasing values of \\\\(y_k\\\\), and therefore of \\\\(n_k\\\\), make the pairs \\\\((m_k,n_k)\\\\) distinct. Consequently there are infinitely many such pairs of positive integers.\"}]}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
